abstract: >-
  A pre-registered test of whether the free-text observing notes astronomers write on TESS
  Objects of Interest contain disposition-relevant information not already present in the
  numeric catalogue columns. Free text is converted to numeric semantic features with a
  System One model (Jev, TypeSafe AI) used strictly as a featurizer; gradient boosting is
  the predictor and cross-validation grouped by host star is the arbiter. Model
  probabilities are never thresholded into a decision.

  On 1,482 labelled TOIs across 1,388 host stars, semantic features improve held-out
  prediction of TFOPWG disposition by dAUC +0.0432 (95% CI +0.0324 to +0.0547; AUC 0.9044
  to 0.9476), which is 5.3 times the minimum effect the study was pre-registered to detect.
  All six pre-registered gates pass, including stability under paraphrase, a temporal
  split, a leakage-stripped arm and a missingness ablation. The gain survives a metadata
  control that captures note count, note length and author identity (+0.0220, CI +0.0126
  to +0.0321), so it is not merely a record of which follow-up group wrote a note. A
  TF-IDF baseline on the same text scores 0.8766, BELOW the numeric baseline, so whatever
  the semantic features capture, bag-of-words does not reach it.

  Two label-leakage findings are reported as cautions for anyone applying machine learning
  to these catalogues. The ExoFOP Comments field restates the disposition in 47.9% of
  2,725 labelled rows, where a TF-IDF classifier reaches 0.969 AUC almost entirely from
  label echo; this is why the study moved to observer notes. Separately, membership in the
  NASA Exoplanet Archive pscomppars table predicts the label at P=0.995 versus 0.074,
  leaking through missingness alone.

  A separately pre-registered transfer test applied the same design to 4,720 Kepler Objects
  of Interest, using the Kepler Community Follow-up Observing Program's observer notes. The
  semantic features again beat the numeric covariates (dAUC +0.0234, CI +0.0186 to +0.0282),
  but a control that makes no model call and only counts note volume and authorship beat
  the features (D minus B+meta = -0.0023, CI -0.0041 to -0.0004), and three of six features
  failed the paraphrase-stability gate. By the pre-registered rule the content claim does
  not transfer, and the TESS result should be read as specific to ExoFOP-TESS observer notes.

  Scope and limits are stated rather than implied. The label is a human judgment by the
  TESS Follow-up Observing Program and the notes are the evidence record produced on the
  way to it, so the study measures whether a model can anticipate expert consensus from
  follow-up prose -- not whether it can detect planets. The TESS result rests on one corpus,
  one snapshot date and one pinned model version (jev-1.13.0), did not transfer to Kepler,
  and has not been externally replicated. The study was designed so that a null result would be
  publishable; the pre-registered prior predicted a null and was falsified, which is
  reported rather than dropped. Pre-registration, an append-only work log, a full account
  of a defect that moved the published headline DOWN by 0.0008, and a measured cold-cache
  reproduction are all included in the repository. Version 1.1.0 also corrects a concurrency
  defect in the paraphrase-stability script, which had overstated the paraphrase-to-noise
  ratio (reported 412x, actually 4.4x) and the model's run-to-run stability; no gate verdict
  changed.
